Z||000005||Monkeyman||01-27-2000||04:11 AM||||Aren't the ST springs the ones from JCWhitney?  I've always considered JCWhitney to be sort of a Kmart of parts (cheap and most of the time cheaply made) but I keep hearing about these springs.  What difference would there be between lowering springs and dropped spindles?  (Other than the cost.)  I could probably do an exhaustive search and figure it out for myself, but it's 4am and I'm just too damn tired to do that.||||reg||1
Z||000006||william_thorniii||01-27-2000||09:22 AM||||Monkey,<P>I will touch on the spindles vs. springs.  The advantage of spindles is that your suspension works as it was intended to.  Everything stays in the same place relative to one another except for the wheel.  Therefore if you want serious handling without serious modifications most will look into spindles.  Springs are an easy way to lower the vehicle and can also be the first step in a complete suspension re-engineering.  The main criticism of the springs comes from those looking for the ultimate performance of their suspension.  Since each of the individual components are in a slightly different position relative to each other, it can cause problems.  On some vehicles the problems can be rather severe.  Most springs now do not cause major problems though.<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||10
Z||000007||Tom88gt||01-27-2000||01:30 PM||||There is no problem with the quality of JC Whitney stuff......<P>...as long as you know what the brand and model is.  Yes JC carries a lot of cheap stuff.  But they also carry some first quality, name-brand stuff at good prices.  I spent 2 years on Crete and I kept my CJ-7 alive solely because of JC Whitney.  You would be surprized at what they sell.<P>Its not who you buy it from, it what you buy from them.  Of course price and service are always important.  I've never had a problem with a return.||||reg||14
Z||000008||Monkeyman||01-27-2000||03:44 PM||||OK.  Here's what I want to do.  I'm about to get an '85 GT.  I'm sure it'll need some new susp parts.  I want to put coil overs on the back (I might actually be able to afford those!) to lower it and improve the handling.  I have no intention of autoxing the car, but I would like to feel that I could stay with most other cars in the corners.  (At least anything that costs less than the Nat'l Debt!)  Would I be better off (meaning less $$) to go with some sport/lowering springs or do I really need the more expensive dropped spindles?  I plan to upgrade to either 16" or 17" wheels with reasonable quality rubber.  I don't want to have to replace the tires every 6 months.||||reg||1
Z||000009||william_thorniii||01-27-2000||04:24 PM||||Monkey,<P>Street Dreams in MI, has a set of spindles on their site for $295/set.  The price is VERY attractive to me, but they are not available... yet.  They are still in the prototype stage and no production date has been set.  So perhaps after you get the engine installed they will be al ready to go.  I am hoping to get some for the soon to be new to me 86 SE.<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||10
